From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] iommu: sun60i: one page table for both instances

[Recipe cut: the board proof below ran with the vendor video engine and
test-only patches on top; neither is carried in this series, the code is
unchanged.]

The video engine sits on both IOMMUs: the decoder
(video-codec@1c0e000) is IOMMU1 masters 0 and 1, the encoder
(video-codec@1c10000) IOMMU0 master 3. Until this patch each instance had
its own iommu group, domain and page table.

MEASURED -- the 6 GiB board, 2026-09-26, test kernel 6.18.53-t590 (this
series as of that date plus a cedar_ve DMA-mask fix), test dtb with
iommus = <&iommu1 0>, <&iommu1 1> on the
decoder, <&iommu0 3> on the encoder.

  * Decoder behind IOMMU1, in the display engine's group: H.264 decode 120
    of 120 frames md5-identical to software decode, VE interrupts counted.
  * Encoder behind IOMMU0, in a group of its own: no encoder interrupt, and
    "sun60i-a733-iommu 3900000.iommu: L1 page table fault: iova 0xfc203000,
    masters 0x8, data 0x0" -- master 3, the encoder.
  * Why, from an LD_PRELOAD shim logging the buffer ioctls: the closed
    encoder library maps EVERY buffer through the decoder's node -- 16
    IOCTL_GET_IOMMU_ADDR (0x502) on /dev/cedar_dev, none on
    /dev/cedar_dev_ve2; e.g. fd 22 -> IOVA 0xfc200000 in IOMMU1's domain.
    The encoder then uses that IOVA on IOMMU0, whose table does not map it.

The vendor driver writes every global register, TTB included, to both
blocks (WRITE_EQUAL_REG): one page table for both, and
the closed user space depends on that one IOVA space. An encoder behind
IOMMU0 with a table of its own cannot work with it; a translating decoder
beside an encoder in bypass is worse, the encoder would take IOVAs as
physical DRAM addresses. The group per instance was this driver's choice,
not the hardware's: each block walks the table its own TTB names (IOMMU1
translating while IOMMU0 read TTB 0, 2026-09-18), so two TTBs can name one
table.

MAINLINE PRECEDENT

  * mtk_iommu.c, SHARE_PGTABLE ("2 HW share pgtable"): device_group hands
    out one group across instances (m4u_group of the first instance on the
    shared hw_list), attach writes the one table into each instance's base
    register at its first attach, a range flush walks every instance of the
    hw_list.
  * rockchip-iommu.c: one domain in several register blocks, each
    invalidation (rk_iommu_zap_iova()) run on every IOMMU the domain is
    attached to.

WHAT CHANGES

  * device_group: sun60i_iommu_device_group() replaces
    generic_single_device_group(). One group for the masters of both
    instances, allocated on first use and held for the life of the driver,
    as generic_single_device_group() holds its singleton. The core allocates
    one default domain per group, so the display engine, the decoder and the
    encoder share one DMA domain: one IOVA space, one table.
  * attach: the refusal of a domain allocated for a device of the other
    instance (dom->iommu != iommu -> -EINVAL) is gone. Nothing else in
    attach changes: an instance's TTB is written and the instance enabled
    when its first master attaches (sun60i_iommu_hw_enable(): TTB, flush,
    ENABLE), and it is disabled when its last master leaves.
  * invalidation: iotlb_sync_map, iotlb_sync and flush_iotlb_all call
    sun60i_iommu_domain_invalidate(). It runs the flush_all() flush -- +0x030, then
    +0x18 of every master with a user -- on every instance whose TTB points
    at the domain, one instance after the other, each under its own lock.
    Instances are on a driver-wide list: added in probe once their clocks
    are on and VERSION has answered, before iommu_device_register(), which
    already attaches their devices; removed only if that fails. Lock order:
    list lock, then instance lock; no path in the driver takes them the
    other way round.
  * dom->iommu keeps one job: its device is the one the tables are
    DMA-mapped against, for the cache maintenance. Either instance would
    do: neither node is dma-coherent, both have the 34-bit mask, and the
    sync cleans the CPU caches to DRAM, which both walkers read.
  * of_xlate: the "masters on both IOMMUs" refusal stays, per device, with
    its reason corrected. It said a domain would need two tables; it no
    longer does. What remains: a device's masters are counted on one
    instance (client->iommu; the fwspec ids are master numbers within it).
    The one engine with masters on both is two devices here, as in the
    vendor DT. Lifting it needs a per-id instance in the client and an
    attach across two instance locks; this change does not need it.

WHAT STAYS PER INSTANCE, AND WHY -- decided field by field

  * iommu->domain, the table this instance's TTB names: the core moves a
    group to a new domain one device at a time, so for that moment the two
    instances serve different domains.
  * users[] and master_clk[]: per (instance, master), as the hardware is.
    Attach and detach count on the device's own instance: an attach on
    IOMMU1 never takes or drops an IOMMU0 master's port gate, and a gate is
    released once, by the last user of that master.
  * the lock: it serialises one block's registers.
  * the interrupt handler: it reads and clears its own INT_STA, L1PG_INT
    and L2PG_INT, resets only its own masters with a user, and invalidates
    a faulting IOVA on its own instance. A stale L1PG_INT bit still
    names a master of the same instance, never one of the other.
    report_iommu_fault() now goes to the shared domain, with the faulting
    instance's device.
  * resume (iommu-sun60i-program-the-block-again-on-resume): unchanged.
    Each instance programs itself from the domain its own TTB points at --
    TTB, flush, ENABLE, then its masters with a user -- so both get the same
    table address.

Moved from the instance to the driver: the group, and the list of instances
an invalidation runs on.

GATED BLOCKS AND PM

A register access to a gated block stalls the bus, so a flush
over several instances must not reach one. It does not: an instance joins
the list after its four clocks are on, the driver never gates them (they are
CLK_IS_CRITICAL too) and has no runtime PM; per instance the flush writes
+0x030 and the +0x18 of masters with a user, whose port gate is held. An
instance whose TTB does not point at the domain is not touched. No
"suspended" state skips instances between suspend_late and resume_early:
the display engine has no PM callbacks and scans through a system sleep, so
a skipped flush would leave a stale translation live for it. Reprogramming
after a state loss stays with resume_early.

STILL REJECTED FROM THE VENDOR MODEL

  * one DT node for both blocks, every global register written to both
    whether or not it translates: two nodes here, each instance programmed
    only while it has a master with a user (IOMMU0 without one stays at
    ENABLE 0, TTB 0);
  * translation switched from a DT cell with empty attach/detach: attach
    and identity per master with user counts, BYP per master;
  * overwriting a live entry: map still returns -EBUSY;
  * invalidating before clearing, and the range command +0x054: clear, then
    global flush, then per-master flush, per instance;
  * one global domain: domain_alloc_paging still returns a fresh domain per
    call. What is shared is the group, hence the default domain.

WHAT IT COSTS

Isolation. DE0 (IOMMU1 m2) already shared its table with the decoder behind
IOMMU1; now every IOMMU0 master with "iommus" joins -- today only the
encoder (arm64-dts-a733-ve-behind-the-iommus). The video engine can reach
every IOVA mapped for the display engine, and the reverse. The
vendor user space needs exactly that, and it is still narrower than bypass,
where the engine reaches all of DRAM. A buffer unmapped through one device
while a master behind the other instance still uses its IOVA faults on that
other instance.
Time. With users on both instances every sync flushes both: two +0x030 and
one +0x18 per master with a user, each dropping by the first read, about
3.5 us.

HARDWARE-PROVEN -- the 6 GiB board, 2026-09-26, test kernel 6.18.53-t590b
(this series with arm64-dts-a733-ve-behind-the-iommus and cedar_ve's 32-bit
mask behind an IOMMU), arm b:
  * one iommu_group: 1c0e000.video-codec, 1c10000.video-codec,
    5100000.mixer, display-engine;
  * TTB (+0x020) 0x00040508 on both IOMMUs, ENABLE 1 on both; IOMMU0 m3 and
    IOMMU1 m0/m1/m2 BYP 0 INT_EN 1, every other master BYP 1;
  * the encoder: +300 interrupts, 299 of 300 frames (the tail loss is separate: it is
    the same in bypass), and the decoder decodes that stream 299/299
    md5-identical to software; no fault line from either IOMMU;
  * decode 120/120 md5-identical on two more streams; an address bent to
    an unmapped IOVA faults on IOMMU1 (8 lines, master 0), no oops, and the
    next decode is identical again;
  * decoder and encoder used and released in turn three times, engines
    runtime-suspended, 300 display unmaps after each (each flushing both
    instances): no stall, MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING 0xF1015803 (bits 12/14 held),
    both TTBs unchanged.
Not re-run: a test-only patch (iommu-sun60i-lose-state-across-system-sleep, not carried) on both
instances. The resume code is unchanged, and s2idle keeps the block's state
on this board, so the lost state it simulates does not occur here.

FALSIFIER: a fault on IOMMU0 at an IOVA the decoder mapped while both TTBs
read the same value; the two TTBs differing while both instances have a
master with a user; decoder and encoder in two iommu_groups.

VERIFIED BEFORE THE BOARD (2026-09-26)

Applies at fuzz 0 in the series: 230 of 230 on a freshly unpacked 6.18.53,
both test-only IOMMU patches unchanged on top.
drivers/iommu/sun60i-iommu.o builds for arm64 at W=1 with no warning,
.config from build.sh configure, test options off and on (A733_TEST_KERNEL=1:
SUN60I_IOMMU_CMD_SELFTEST=y, SUN60I_IOMMU_PM_SELFTEST=y -- both
are CONFIG_A733_TEST_INSTRUMENTS); a planted W=1
warning is reported (cross gcc 16.2.1). checkpatch --strict -f on the
driver file: 0/0/0. The object carries sun60i_iommu_device_group and
sun60i_iommu_domain_invalidate. Object only: no Image, no lockdep run.

Not in the unposted v7.3-rc4 version of the driver (see
iommu-sun60i-add-the-allwinner-a733-iommu-driver): the in-tree driver now
differs from it by this patch as well.

Assisted-by: Claude

